    "html": "<p><strong>Accrual accounting records revenue and expenses in the periods to which they belong under the accounting framework, rather than using cash dates alone. Separate the delivery, billing and settlement evidence before comparing profit with a bank balance.</strong></p><p>All amounts and operating results below are original fictional teaching examples in USD. They are not customer results, industry averages or forecasts. Use your own dated records and applicable accounting policies.</p><h2 id=\"recognition\">Identify what happened before choosing the date</h2><p>For this introductory financial-reporting example, assume an accepted service gives an unconditional right to payment and all relevant recognition conditions are satisfied in November. The invoice is issued later. The invoice date by itself does not determine when revenue is earned. If payment depends on additional performance, a contract asset or another treatment may be relevant; do not automatically call every unbilled amount accounts receivable.</p><p>Likewise, distinguish a service consumed, an asset purchased and an advance paid. A December payment for November work can settle an existing obligation. Insurance paid in advance can provide coverage over several periods. These distinctions organize the evidence; they are not an election of a permissible US tax method.</p><h2 id=\"dates\">Use four transactions with separate accounting and cash dates</h2><div class=\"article-table-scroll\"><table><caption>Original November 2026 accrual case; USD</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Event</th><th scope=\"col\">November recognition</th><th scope=\"col\">Cash / billing date</th><th scope=\"col\">Illustrative posting</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Accepted November service; unconditional payment right</th><td>$5,000 earned in November</td><td>Invoice Dec 1; collect Dec 15</td><td>Dr receivable / Cr revenue 5,000</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">November payroll earned</th><td>$1,800 November expense</td><td>Pay December 5</td><td>Dr wage expense / Cr payable 1,800</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Insurance paid November 1; twelve months coverage</th><td>$100 November expense; $1,100 prepaid</td><td>$1,200 November payment</td><td>Dr insurance expense / Cr prepaid 100 at close</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Customer advance for December service</th><td>No November revenue from this advance</td><td>$2,400 November receipt</td><td>Dr cash / Cr contract liability 2,400</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The insurance initially creates a $1,200 prepaid asset. One month of coverage is then consumed, leaving $1,100. The $2,400 advance is assumed refundable or deliverable under the contract and wholly unearned at November 30. It is not extra November sales merely because money arrived. No sales tax, bad-debt adjustment, depreciation or other expenses are included in this deliberately limited example.</p><h2 id=\"bridge\">Reconcile November profit, cash and balances</h2><p>Earned revenue is $5,000. Included expenses are $1,800 payroll plus $100 insurance, so accrual profit is <strong>$3,100</strong>. Operating receipts less payments in this case are $2,400 − $1,200 = $1,200. The $1,900 difference between profit and this operating cash movement is explained by $5,000 new receivable, $1,100 remaining prepaid, $1,800 unpaid wages and $2,400 customer advance: $3,100 − $5,000 − $1,100 + $1,800 + $2,400 = $1,200.</p><p>Add a separate $10,000 owner cash investment at the beginning. Closing cash becomes $11,200, not $3,100. Assets are $11,200 cash + $5,000 receivable + $1,100 prepaid = $17,300. Liabilities are $1,800 wages + $2,400 advance = $4,200. Equity is $10,000 investment + $3,100 profit = $13,100. Thus $17,300 = $4,200 + $13,100.</p><p>This is a financial-reporting illustration. A simple cash receipts/payments view of these transactions is not a complete tax-basis income computation. Loan principal, equipment purchases, deposits, constructive receipt and applicable tax rules can require additional analysis.</p><h2 id=\"settle\">Carry the balances into December without counting twice</h2><p>On December 15, collecting the $5,000 reduces the receivable and increases cash; it does not recognize the same service revenue again. Paying $1,800 wages settles the November payable. Consuming another month of insurance reduces the prepaid asset by another $100. Recognize the advance as earned only when the assumed December service conditions are satisfied, not automatically because the calendar changed.</p><p>Preserve references from each settlement to its original transaction. Use <a href=\"/blogs/accounts-receivable-process/\">the receivable workflow</a> for collection allocation and <a href=\"/blogs/current-liabilities/\">the liability schedule</a> for unpaid obligations. A balanced entry can still contain the wrong period or classification.</p><h2 id=\"close\">Use a cutoff review before reading the result</h2><p>At month end, inspect accepted work, invoices issued before or after cutoff, services received but unbilled, unused prepayments and unfulfilled customer promises. Document the reporting basis and reviewer. Reconcile opening balances plus movements to closing schedules; do not replace missing evidence with an arbitrary journal to make profit look reasonable.</p><p>The <a href=\"/blogs/accounting-cycle/\">accounting cycle</a> connects adjustments to statements. <a href=\"/blogs/bookkeeping-small-business/\">Bookkeeping for small business</a> covers the routine evidence collection, and the <a href=\"/calculators/cash-flow-forecast-template/\">cash forecast</a> handles future payment timing. IRS Publication 538 is the separate official reference for tax accounting methods; this guide makes no blanket eligibility or change-of-method claim.</p><h2 id=\"worksheet\">Download the editable worksheet</h2><p><a href=\"/downloads/accrual-accounting-worksheet.html\" download>Download the printable HTML worksheet</a>. It contains the completed case and a separate blank working record. It does not calculate automatically. Edit in your browser, then print or save a completed copy before closing; your edits are not sent to MyBreakeven.</p><h2 id=\"sources\">Sources and scope</h2><p>Primary references below support the stated definitions or framework. The worked case, numbers and decision records are original MyBreakeven material. Reviewed November 7, 2026; no external professional sign-off is claimed.</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/principles-financial-accounting/pages/4-2-discuss-the-adjustment-process-and-illustrate-common-types-of-adjusting-entries\">OpenStax: adjusting-entry framework</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p538\">IRS Publication 538: separate tax accounting-method guidance</a></li></ul>",
