    "html": "<p><strong>The accounting cycle connects transaction evidence to journal entries, ledger accounts, adjustments, statements and closing. Keep the unadjusted, adjusted and post-closing balances separate so each stage has a traceable purpose.</strong></p><p>All amounts and operating results below are original fictional teaching examples in USD. They are not customer results, industry averages or forecasts. Use your own dated records and applicable accounting policies.</p><h2 id=\"record\">Record six supported events before adjusting</h2><p>This fictional sole-proprietor service business begins with zero balances. The owner invests $10,000 cash. It buys $900 supplies on account, earns and invoices $4,000 of accepted services, collects $2,500 of that receivable, pays $600 rent for the month, and receives $1,200 for future services. No tax, depreciation, inventory for resale or owner drawings are included.</p><p>Journal entries preserve the two-sided effect of each event. For example, supplies purchased on account debit Supplies and credit Accounts Payable by $900. Collecting $2,500 debits Cash and credits Accounts Receivable; it does not record another sale. Post each journal reference into the appropriate ledger accounts.</p><h2 id=\"adjust\">Keep the adjustment evidence distinct</h2><p>At month end, a supported count shows $600 supplies remain, so $300 has been used. Employees have earned $700 unpaid wages. Of the $1,200 advance, $400 is now earned under the case’s service-recognition assumptions. Record three adjustments: Dr Supplies Expense / Cr Supplies $300; Dr Wages Expense / Cr Wages Payable $700; Dr Customer Advance Liability / Cr Service Revenue $400.</p><p>These entries change the period’s result and balances without creating a cash transaction at the adjustment date. Preserve the count, payroll cutoff and delivery evidence. Adjusting to a desired profit number is not a valid substitute for that support.</p><h2 id=\"trial\">Compare unadjusted and adjusted trial balances</h2><div class=\"article-table-scroll\"><table><caption>Original trial-balance comparison, USD</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Account</th><th scope=\"col\">Unadjusted balance</th><th scope=\"col\">Adjusted balance</th><th scope=\"col\">Normal side</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Cash</th><td>13,100</td><td>13,100</td><td>Debit</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Accounts receivable</th><td>1,500</td><td>1,500</td><td>Debit</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Unused supplies</th><td>900</td><td>600</td><td>Debit</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Rent expense</th><td>600</td><td>600</td><td>Debit</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Supplies expense</th><td>0</td><td>300</td><td>Debit</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Wages expense</th><td>0</td><td>700</td><td>Debit</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Accounts payable</th><td>900</td><td>900</td><td>Credit</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Wages payable</th><td>0</td><td>700</td><td>Credit</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Customer advance liability</th><td>1,200</td><td>800</td><td>Credit</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Owner capital</th><td>10,000</td><td>10,000</td><td>Credit</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Service revenue</th><td>4,000</td><td>4,400</td><td>Credit</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Debit / credit totals</th><td>16,100 each</td><td>16,800 each</td><td>Balanced</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The unadjusted trial balance totals $16,100 on each side. After adjustments, both sides total <strong>$16,800</strong>. Equal totals show posting equality, not proof that the right accounts and periods were used. An omitted event or a wrong but balanced entry can escape this test.</p><h2 id=\"statements\">Prepare statements from the adjusted balances</h2><p>Recognized revenue is $4,400. Expenses are $600 rent + $300 supplies + $700 wages = $1,600, leaving $2,800 profit. Closing equity before any drawings is $10,000 investment + $2,800 profit = $12,800. Assets are $13,100 cash + $1,500 receivables + $600 supplies = $15,200. Liabilities are $900 AP + $700 wages + $800 unearned advance = $2,400. The balance sheet reconciles: $15,200 = $2,400 + $12,800.</p><p>Cash increased $13,100 from zero, which differs from profit because owner funding, collections, credit purchases and the customer advance have different effects. The cash-flow statement and reporting framework require their own classification; do not copy the profit subtotal into a cash balance.</p><h2 id=\"closing\">Close temporary accounts without deleting history</h2><p>For this illustrative sole-proprietor closing convention, debit Revenue $4,400 and credit Income Summary. Debit Income Summary $1,600 and credit the three expense accounts. Transfer the remaining $2,800 credit from Income Summary to Owner Capital. Revenue, expenses and Income Summary then have zero balances; capital is $12,800. There are no drawings to close in this case.</p><p>The post-closing trial balance contains $15,200 debit assets and $15,200 credit liabilities/equity. Real systems may automate closing or use different equity accounts; do not post these manual entries on top of an automated close. Preserve the transaction history and the reports used for approval.</p><h2 id=\"review\">Use each report for its own question</h2><p>The journal explains what was recorded; <a href=\"/blogs/general-ledger-template/\">the ledger</a> groups postings by account. The trial balance checks aggregate equality; the statements report the resulting position and performance. <a href=\"/blogs/bookkeeping-small-business/\">The bookkeeping routine</a> gathers evidence and reconciles detail. <a href=\"/blogs/accrual-accounting/\">Accrual accounting</a> explains why dates can differ.</p><p>Keep the prior period’s closing balances as the next period’s opening balances and record correction references. Use <a href=\"/blogs/how-to-analyze-financial-statements/\">statement analysis</a> after the close to interpret the result, rather than treating interpretation as another posting step.</p><h2 id=\"worksheet\">Download the editable worksheet</h2><p><a href=\"/downloads/accounting-cycle-worksheet.html\" download>Download the printable HTML worksheet</a>. It contains the completed case and a separate blank working record. It does not calculate automatically. Edit in your browser, then print or save a completed copy before closing; your edits are not sent to MyBreakeven.</p><h2 id=\"sources\">Sources and scope</h2><p>Primary references below support the stated definitions or framework. The worked case, numbers and decision records are original MyBreakeven material. Reviewed November 7, 2026; no external professional sign-off is claimed.</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/principles-financial-accounting/pages/4-2-discuss-the-adjustment-process-and-illustrate-common-types-of-adjusting-entries\">OpenStax: adjustment process and trial-balance stages</a></li><li><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/principles-financial-accounting/pages/5-1-describe-and-prepare-closing-entries-for-a-business\">OpenStax: closing-entry sequence</a></li></ul>",
